  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss and capped at the rim. It is steeper in x, so steps overshoot there first. Rates above 2/3 make the x swings grow until the run diverges.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with SD 1.5·√((64−b)/(63b)), so it is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. Noise is fixed by the run and step number.",
    "Arrows are drawn at their true step length (scale 1, in bowl units). The orange arrow is the mini-batch step the ball will take. The grey arrow is −learning rate × the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn square. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Reset replays the same noise from run 0. New run keeps the last path as the ghost and uses the next noise values."
  ]
}